1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,540
Man, the ocean's greatest predator.

2
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:12,320
He's developed the ships and the science to catch every fish in the sea.

3
00:00:13,780 --> 00:00:15,240
Each ship is different.

4
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:17,620
Each fish, a unique challenge.

5
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:21,900
In the Bering Sea, there is a fish that literally feeds the world.

6
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:23,200
Alaska Pollock.

7
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:26,140
And it takes a ship as big as a factory to land it.

8
00:00:26,580 --> 00:00:27,520
Alaska Ocean.

9
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:31,980
Its half-mile-long nets pull in hauls 100 tons at a time.

10
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:33,220
Yeah, it was a good haul.

11
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:36,100
Stuffed a bin. We got some on deck. It's good.

12
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:38,900
Below deck, the ship's state-of-the-art factory

13
00:00:38,901 --> 00:00:42,940
processes 750 fish per minute, 24-7.

14
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:44,960
If you walk into a McDonald's,

15
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:48,660
there's a very good chance that the Filet-O-Fish came from this vessel.

16
00:00:48,661 --> 00:00:51,180
The season is short. The clock is ticking.

17
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:55,440
The captain and his crew must keep this vast array of high-tech in motion

18
00:00:55,441 --> 00:00:58,460
to harvest their quota, 22,000 tons.

19
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:00,240
It's a needle in a haystack kind of a thing.

20
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:02,000
There's so many square miles of water,

21
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:03,720
and this net is relatively small.

22
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:06,060
Will they make their catch

23
00:01:06,061 --> 00:01:09,120
or be bested by the most treacherous seas on the planet?

24
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:11,060
Many, many of my best friends

25
00:01:11,061 --> 00:01:12,960
are on the bottom of the ocean up here.

26
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:15,920
Come aboard Alaska Ocean

27
00:01:15,921 --> 00:01:17,460
as it hunts the Bering Sea

28
00:01:17,461 --> 00:01:20,160
for the fish that everybody eats but nobody knows.

29
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:23,560
Now, Alaska Pollock Fishing on Modern Marvel.

30
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:39,440
The Bering Sea, west of Alaska and east of Siberia.

31
00:01:41,980 --> 00:01:47,440
Beneath its waters, the flat contours of the Bering Shelf drop off thousands of feet into

32
00:01:47,441 --> 00:01:49,820
the frigid Aleutian Basin.

33
00:01:49,821 --> 00:01:54,380
It's the topography that creates the richest fishing grounds in the world.

34
00:01:54,381 --> 00:02:02,140
To the south, on the Aleutian Islands, you'll find Dutch Harbor.

35
00:02:02,141 --> 00:02:04,260
It's America's number one fishing port.

36
00:02:04,261 --> 00:02:08,620
The pride of the Dutch Harbor fleet is Alaska Ocean.

37
00:02:08,621 --> 00:02:14,260
Half fishing boat, half floating factory, it processes and flash freezes Alaska Pollock

38
00:02:14,261 --> 00:02:16,660
as fast as their cod.

39
00:02:16,661 --> 00:02:22,500
Alaska Ocean's crew of 130 trust their livelihoods and their lives to Captain Scott Simons.

40
00:02:22,501 --> 00:02:25,100
I've been on board the Alaska Ocean for 17 years.

41
00:02:25,101 --> 00:02:27,200
I've been a fisherman my whole adult life.

42
00:02:27,201 --> 00:02:29,000
I actually started when I was in high school,

43
00:02:29,001 --> 00:02:31,500
going salmon fishing in the summertime.

44
00:02:31,501 --> 00:02:34,500
I've been the captain on board now since 1998.

45
00:02:34,501 --> 00:02:36,500
Prior to that, I was a mate,

46
00:02:36,501 --> 00:02:39,500
and I was the first deck boss on the boat.

47
00:02:39,501 --> 00:02:42,000
Scott's responsibilities?

48
00:02:42,001 --> 00:02:45,500
First and foremost is operating the vessel safely.

49
00:02:45,501 --> 00:02:47,500
Secondarily is finding fish.

50
00:02:47,501 --> 00:02:52,500
At 3.30 a.m. on August 10th,

51
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:57,500
the crew is ready to cast off

52
00:02:57,501 --> 00:03:00,160
and head for the fishing grounds on the last trip

53
00:03:00,161 --> 00:03:02,300
of the grueling four-month summer season.

54
00:03:02,860 --> 00:03:04,700
Take the stern line off, Franz.

55
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:08,960
The skipper can't afford to wait for first light.

56
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:12,200
He'll need every minute at sea to catch the ship's quota.

57
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:15,020
Hey, Eric, you need to stay down forward there for the anchor, please.

58
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:16,820
Storm petrol, Alaska ocean.

59
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:19,000
We're outbound here, port to port.

60
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:23,420
So when we get outside here and clear Elatka Head,

61
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:26,000
which is the landmass off to our port side here,

62
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:28,700
we'll just turn northwest and head up toward the general area

63
00:03:28,701 --> 00:03:29,680
where we're going to start fishing.

64
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:36,260
Scott plots his course to the edge of the U.S. Economic Zone,

65
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:38,880
500 miles north in the Bering Sea,

66
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:43,180
notorious for the most violent weather and ocean conditions on the planet.

67
00:03:43,181 --> 00:03:51,000
When frigid Arctic winds collide with warm air pushed northward by the jet stream, bad weather happens.

68
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:59,700
Though deep chasms rim the Bering Sea off the continental shelf, for the most part, it's a relatively shallow basin.

69
00:04:01,180 --> 00:04:06,860
This allows winds to whip up seas of confused waves that can batter a ship from every direction.

70
00:04:06,861 --> 00:04:12,080
conditions on the Bering Sea can change on a whim producing hurricane force

71
00:04:12,081 --> 00:04:18,400
winds and five-story waves capable of swamping even large ships

72
00:04:19,860 --> 00:04:26,240
that was the case with the anyo maru a 190 foot Japanese factory trawler that

73
00:04:26,241 --> 00:04:32,300
went down off Attic Island amid towering waves in 1999 taking 12 lives with her

74
00:04:32,301 --> 00:04:39,400
Even on a boat this size, you know, we could take water up the stern, and that's what sinks a lot of these boats is the down flooding.

75
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:45,580
They'll have a hatch open, a doorway open, and water comes up the stern and down floods the boat, and it doesn't take long at all.

76
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:50,920
Anybody that's been in the business long enough, you lose quite a few of your friends.

77
00:04:54,660 --> 00:05:00,840
As Alaska Ocean steams towards her fishing grounds, her crew can face the challenges of the Bering Sea,

78
00:05:00,841 --> 00:05:05,300
knowing that they're aboard one of the strongest, safest fishing vessels in the world.

79
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:08,980
She's longer than a football field and six stories tall.

80
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:16,500
A 6,250-horsepower engine drives the ship through mountainous waves.

81
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:22,440
Her 13-and-a-half-foot diameter propeller powers the boat at speeds up to 14 knots,

82
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:25,260
and her hull is armed with thick steel reinforcements

83
00:05:25,261 --> 00:05:28,220
to withstand even the worst poundings of Arctic storms.

84
00:05:28,221 --> 00:05:33,220
Two rescue boats flank the trawl deck, where nets are hauled and stowed.

85
00:05:33,221 --> 00:05:39,220
And overlooking everything is the wheelhouse, the ship's nerve center, where all operations are supervised.

86
00:05:41,220 --> 00:05:46,220
This voyage, Alaska Ocean must catch over 12 million pounds of Alaska Pollock

87
00:05:46,221 --> 00:05:51,220
to fulfill its quota of 22,000 tons for the season.

88
00:05:58,220 --> 00:06:02,880
To find the fish, Scott needs the very latest sonar and tracking equipment available

89
00:06:02,881 --> 00:06:04,880
as he begins scouting polyp.

90
00:06:07,860 --> 00:06:11,060
I have a couple of different chart plotters that are hooked up to GPS,

91
00:06:11,360 --> 00:06:14,440
global positions, means satellites, and that gives us our exact position.

92
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:17,880
These chart plotters show the bathymetric lines of the ocean floor,

93
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:19,360
so we see the depth contours.

94
00:06:19,620 --> 00:06:21,360
It's kind of like a topographical map.

95
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:24,500
This area we're next to here now is called Zemchug Canyon,

96
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:26,640
and we're going to go on the north side of Zemchug.

97
00:06:26,641 --> 00:06:35,580
Zemshug Canyon, which drops off precipitously from 600 to 8,500 feet, is the largest underwater

98
00:06:35,581 --> 00:06:40,680
canyon in the world, bigger even than its dryland counterpart, the Grand Canyon.

99
00:06:40,681 --> 00:06:46,420
This area here is relatively unstable for fishing because the fish are constantly moving,

100
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:50,720
going up shallower to feed or maybe out deeper off the edge to feed.

101
00:06:51,380 --> 00:06:53,620
I don't particularly care to fish there in that area very often.

102
00:06:53,621 --> 00:06:56,920
and these red crosses and things are snags or bad bottom.

103
00:06:57,140 --> 00:06:58,260
They're possibly shipwrecks.

104
00:06:58,640 --> 00:07:00,600
There's thousands of them up here in the Bering Sea.

105
00:07:00,780 --> 00:07:06,540
Since 1741, when Russian fur traders first came to the Bering Sea seeking otter pelts,

106
00:07:06,780 --> 00:07:10,500
treacherous Alaskan waters have claimed over 2,500 ships.

107
00:07:11,020 --> 00:07:14,380
19th century whalers, Japanese World War II subs,

108
00:07:14,381 --> 00:07:20,320
and crab boats that went down this year lay on the bottom just waiting to snag Alaska Ocean's nets.

109
00:07:20,321 --> 00:07:23,820
It's kind of dangerous to tow right here.

110
00:07:23,821 --> 00:07:29,020
If it was good fish indication there, then I'd probably consider making a tow and doing

111
00:07:29,021 --> 00:07:30,020
that.

112
00:07:30,021 --> 00:07:35,080
By fish indication, Scott is referring to the signals received on his down sounder

113
00:07:35,081 --> 00:07:38,520
that allow him to see beneath his ship.

114
00:07:38,521 --> 00:07:43,380
A sonar device mounted on the ship's hull sends out an acoustic signal.

115
00:07:43,381 --> 00:07:48,960
It strikes the seabed and gets reflected back and is received.

116
00:07:48,961 --> 00:07:51,200
The fish reflect the sonar too.

117
00:07:51,201 --> 00:07:58,840
The air in their swim bladder slows the acoustic signal, making them visible to the echo sounder.

118
00:07:58,841 --> 00:08:03,320
These two are echo sounders and there is a little bit of fish down there right now.

119
00:08:03,321 --> 00:08:06,920
The more they come together, the denser it is, the darker the color is.

120
00:08:06,921 --> 00:08:10,280
And usually they're very dense when they're schooled together like that and that's obviously

121
00:08:10,281 --> 00:08:12,520
what we're looking for.

122
00:08:12,521 --> 00:08:16,720
Catching his crew off guard, Scott makes an unexpected call.

123
00:08:16,721 --> 00:08:21,120
Hey, Dave, you want to fire him up, please, and hook up a net?

124
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:36,520
The half a mile of lines, net, and gear set up behind the boat costs over a half a million dollars.

125
00:08:37,180 --> 00:08:40,200
Losing it is something Scott doesn't even want to think about.

126
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:44,540
Oh, we're not superstitious, but we'll knock on wood after we talk about stuff like that.

127
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:46,320
We try not to talk about it.

128
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:54,720
Out on the trawl deck, the hands get ready to set the net.

129
00:08:54,721 --> 00:08:57,720
The wire back there he's holding onto, that's the outhaul.

130
00:08:57,721 --> 00:09:00,720
This is the first thing you will step on when setting the net.

131
00:09:00,721 --> 00:09:05,720
That'll pull the net down the stern ramp, and it'll yank that hook out.

132
00:09:05,721 --> 00:09:10,720
And then I, you know, let out the net as the current pulls it out.

133
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:30,720
Now set the doors up to about five fathoms and off she goes.

134
00:09:31,400 --> 00:09:35,280
The ship trawls at three knots, roughly three and a half miles per hour.

135
00:09:35,281 --> 00:09:41,160
The current pulls the net behind the boat, spreading it out in the shape of a giant cone.

136
00:09:41,161 --> 00:09:45,260
Water passing through the trawled doors opens the net wide, while the weights pull the mouth

137
00:09:45,261 --> 00:09:50,822
of the net down. The third wire, an electronic cable, holds up the top of the net.

138
00:09:50,823 --> 00:09:51,523
It carries

139
00:09:51,524 --> 00:09:57,169
signals from the transducer, a sonar transmitter the crew calls the suitcase.

140
00:09:57,170 --> 00:09:58,200
The transducer

141
00:09:58,201 --> 00:10:03,140
emits an acoustic pulse that reflects off the mouth of the net, capturing an image of

142
00:10:03,141 --> 00:10:06,408
both the shape of the net and the fish entering it.

143
00:10:06,409 --> 00:10:09,100
The net's mesh narrows as it tapers toward

144
00:10:09,101 --> 00:10:18,880
the cod end the bag where fish collect up on the bridge Scott keeps a close eye on signals from the

145
00:10:18,881 --> 00:10:26,920
transducer watching for fish looks like we might have a little tangle right now I'm not sure what's

146
00:10:26,921 --> 00:10:33,580
going on there's something's not quite right but this should not be wavy it should be pretty straight

147
00:10:33,581 --> 00:10:39,940
and I can see the suitcase it has a sensor in it for pitch and roll so you

148
00:10:39,941 --> 00:10:44,500
can see how it's tilted and I'm watching it and the case is going back and forth

149
00:10:44,501 --> 00:10:51,500
so something's tangled up making the top not fly quite straight Scott's got to

150
00:10:51,501 --> 00:10:56,680
make a tough call haul in the net and reset the transponder losing precious

151
00:10:56,681 --> 00:11:02,440
time and possibly as fish or continue fishing blind risking that his net never

152
00:11:02,441 --> 00:11:05,900
opened.

153
00:11:05,901 --> 00:11:11,000
Until the advent of the Pollock factory ship in the 1960s, Alaska Pollock was considered

154
00:11:11,001 --> 00:11:25,001
a trash fish, unworthy of catch.

155
00:11:25,360 --> 00:11:29,700
It's day two aboard Alaska Ocean's final trip of the season.

156
00:11:29,701 --> 00:11:34,680
The first net is set, but there's no video signal from the transponder.

157
00:11:34,681 --> 00:11:38,460
This picture just keeps going back and forth like this, so it makes it very difficult to

158
00:11:38,461 --> 00:11:40,200
see fish going in the net.

159
00:11:40,201 --> 00:11:44,660
It's obviously a less than ideal situation.

160
00:11:44,661 --> 00:11:50,160
With the ship's operating costs running roughly $80,000 a day, Scott can't afford to waste

161
00:11:50,161 --> 00:11:51,160
time.

162
00:11:51,161 --> 00:11:56,000
In a situation like this, without being able to see the net opening very well, we're relying

163
00:11:56,001 --> 00:12:02,480
on our catch indicators through these three here to let us know how much fish

164
00:12:02,481 --> 00:12:07,160
is in the net when it gets full to a certain point Scott can only wait and

165
00:12:07,161 --> 00:12:13,100
hope that the fish are entering his net after 15 minutes Scott gets the signal

166
00:12:13,101 --> 00:12:18,920
he'd hoped for see we just got our first catch indicator it turned from from

167
00:12:18,921 --> 00:12:24,920
yellow to red that should be about 30 tons of fish means the nets fishing so

168
00:12:24,921 --> 00:12:27,020
So that's a good sign.

169
00:12:27,021 --> 00:12:28,620
Stuff's going in OK.

170
00:12:28,621 --> 00:12:29,660
So pretty happy to see that.

171
00:12:33,660 --> 00:12:34,760
Wow, this is Jim.

172
00:12:34,761 --> 00:12:36,400
Yeah, good morning, Jimmy.

173
00:12:36,401 --> 00:12:38,300
Hey, it's going pretty good here.

174
00:12:38,301 --> 00:12:41,800
So I'll probably be hauling back in the next half hour.

175
00:12:41,801 --> 00:12:43,880
OK, Scott.

176
00:12:43,881 --> 00:12:45,780
OK, now we're getting ready to haul back.

177
00:12:45,781 --> 00:12:47,940
Now we just do everything in reverse.

178
00:12:47,941 --> 00:12:49,540
Everything starts coming back up again.

179
00:12:54,920 --> 00:12:58,220
The doors are coming up behind the boat.

180
00:12:58,221 --> 00:13:00,220
The next thing up is the weight chains.

181
00:13:02,720 --> 00:13:07,440
Then we disconnect the transducer, get it out of the way.

182
00:13:07,441 --> 00:13:12,500
Everything's coming up, waiting for the cod end to come up.

183
00:13:12,501 --> 00:13:15,240
The guys on the bridge run the handles for all the hooks

184
00:13:15,241 --> 00:13:17,500
out here to bring the cod end up.

185
00:13:17,501 --> 00:13:19,320
That'll pull the bag all the way up on the deck.

186
00:13:21,820 --> 00:13:24,220
I hope that holds.

187
00:13:24,221 --> 00:13:28,220
Yeah! That's what I'm talking about!

188
00:13:28,221 --> 00:13:30,220
There you go.

189
00:13:30,221 --> 00:13:33,220
Is it? Good.

190
00:13:33,221 --> 00:13:35,220
When the bag is set right over the fish bin,

191
00:13:35,221 --> 00:13:37,220
Brent will pop the zipper,

192
00:13:37,221 --> 00:13:41,220
and all the fish will start coming out going into the bin.

193
00:13:41,221 --> 00:13:44,220
That was our first haulback of this trip.

194
00:13:44,221 --> 00:13:46,220
Went pretty good.

195
00:13:47,220 --> 00:13:50,220
Alaska Pollard, a.k.a. Walleye Pollard,

196
00:13:50,221 --> 00:13:52,220
for their large protruding eyes,

197
00:13:52,221 --> 00:13:57,220
They're a member of the cod family and thrive in the ocean bottoms of the North Pacific.

198
00:13:57,221 --> 00:14:02,220
Copepods, tiny shrimp-like creatures, make up most of their diet.

199
00:14:02,221 --> 00:14:06,220
And though pollock can live up to 15 years, few do.

200
00:14:06,221 --> 00:14:12,220
The big pollock eat the little pollock, and seals and other large fish enjoy a meal of fresh pollock, too.

201
00:14:12,221 --> 00:14:16,220
Fewer than 2% of hatchlings make it to their fourth year.

202
00:14:16,221 --> 00:14:21,220
But Alaska pollock are still one of the most populous fish on the planet.

203
00:14:21,221 --> 00:14:28,520
To sustain the fishery, government scientists only allow 15% of Alaska Pollock to be harvested annually.

204
00:14:29,760 --> 00:14:34,060
That's almost 3 billion pounds of ground weight fish.

205
00:14:34,260 --> 00:14:35,180
That's a lot of fish.

206
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:38,860
So you're looking at a billion pounds plus of finished product.

207
00:14:40,420 --> 00:14:42,140
What becomes of the catch?

208
00:14:43,140 --> 00:14:47,220
Ever eaten frozen fish sticks or a McDonald's Filet-O-Fish sandwich?

209
00:14:47,880 --> 00:14:48,580
Pollock.

210
00:14:48,581 --> 00:14:54,840
If you walk into a McDonald's, there's a very good chance that the Filet-O-Fish came from this vessel.

211
00:14:55,800 --> 00:15:02,600
Pollock's also made into a fish paste called Cerima that's used in over 1,500 fish products in Japan alone.

212
00:15:03,480 --> 00:15:06,940
To catch their fair share of one of the most consumed fish in the world,

213
00:15:07,400 --> 00:15:11,200
Alaska Ocean's crew works around the clock, seven days a week.

214
00:15:11,980 --> 00:15:15,320
Life aboard America's largest fishing boat takes some adjustments.

215
00:15:15,500 --> 00:15:17,120
It's very hard to be out here.

216
00:15:17,121 --> 00:15:22,660
I tell the new people the mental part out here is the toughest the physical you can get used to but

217
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:28,840
The mental of getting used to all the hours being away from home the constant just work work work

218
00:15:29,340 --> 00:15:31,340
Time out here isn't cyclical

219
00:15:31,620 --> 00:15:33,620
There aren't any

220
00:15:33,621 --> 00:15:37,420
TGIF the time out here is linear. It's just one gray line

221
00:15:39,060 --> 00:15:43,840
Despite the drudgery of their jobs the crew of Alaska Ocean is proud of their ship

222
00:15:43,841 --> 00:15:45,840
I consider myself a fisherman

223
00:15:45,841 --> 00:15:50,020
fishermen even though I'm not up there actually hauling the nets in.

224
00:15:50,021 --> 00:15:57,800
And I think everybody on here considers themselves a fisherman from the guy washing the dishes

225
00:15:57,801 --> 00:16:03,780
to the guy down cleaning up the bilges, all the processors, and I think it should be that

226
00:16:03,781 --> 00:16:04,780
way.

227
00:16:04,781 --> 00:16:10,780
To help maintain crew morale, the ship's hotel department provides laundry, cleaning

228
00:16:10,781 --> 00:16:15,340
services, and nonstop meals.

229
00:16:15,341 --> 00:16:18,400
We work 24 hours a day, and there's four meals a day.

230
00:16:19,080 --> 00:16:22,400
There's a lunch, dinner, midnight meal, and then breakfast.

231
00:16:23,200 --> 00:16:24,080
How do you like this at home?

232
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:26,840
At least stay away from the pollock.

233
00:16:27,220 --> 00:16:29,660
No pollock. Try to stay away from the pollock.

234
00:16:30,420 --> 00:16:33,660
I really don't eat much fish at all anymore after being here 12 years.

235
00:16:34,860 --> 00:16:36,860
Crews are well paid on Alaska Ocean.

236
00:16:36,861 --> 00:16:40,680
A deckhand right out of high school can make over $1,000 a week.

237
00:16:41,520 --> 00:16:44,980
I've been on board this boat since I graduated high school last year,

238
00:16:44,981 --> 00:16:47,180
so I skipped my graduation to come up here.

239
00:16:47,900 --> 00:16:48,860
Well, everything's so big.

240
00:16:48,940 --> 00:16:50,360
I mean, it's kind of a whole new world, you know?

241
00:16:50,520 --> 00:16:52,760
It's just a little frightening at first.

242
00:16:54,520 --> 00:16:57,680
Four days into the voyage, things take a turn for the worse.

243
00:17:01,340 --> 00:17:02,700
A storm moves in.

244
00:17:03,340 --> 00:17:05,700
30-mile-an-hour winds and heavy seas.

245
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:09,400
On a day like today, it's hard to see stuff on the echo sounder.

246
00:17:09,480 --> 00:17:12,620
When you're looking for a little tiny thin layer of fish sign on a machine

247
00:17:12,621 --> 00:17:13,500
when you're going up and down,

248
00:17:13,501 --> 00:17:15,400
it's pretty tough for it to pick it up.

249
00:17:15,620 --> 00:17:18,840
So this is probably the worst day of the summer.

250
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:22,620
Not compared to the storms Alaska Ocean encounters

251
00:17:22,621 --> 00:17:26,060
during its winter season, winds get up to 60 knots,

252
00:17:26,520 --> 00:17:30,240
seas crest at over 30 feet, and storms can last for days.

253
00:17:31,340 --> 00:17:33,320
When the deck beneath you is rolling,

254
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:35,180
a hard job gets even harder.

255
00:17:35,820 --> 00:17:37,140
When you get some nasty weather,

256
00:17:37,280 --> 00:17:38,920
I mean, that's where you have a lot of injuries,

257
00:17:39,220 --> 00:17:41,360
100-ton bags and cables breaking.

258
00:17:41,620 --> 00:17:42,820
The boat's rocking.

259
00:17:42,821 --> 00:17:44,780
these blocks come flying out at you.

260
00:17:44,781 --> 00:17:47,420
I've had a whole top of a freezer come out.

261
00:17:47,421 --> 00:17:49,260
It's also harder,

262
00:17:49,261 --> 00:17:51,660
because you're trying to keep your balance and do this,

263
00:17:51,661 --> 00:17:55,460
so it'll make you more tired during the day.

264
00:17:55,461 --> 00:17:58,740
A lot of times you'll be just rocking your body back and forth

265
00:17:58,741 --> 00:18:00,000
just to keep yourself moving,

266
00:18:00,001 --> 00:18:02,380
and these pans will shift real hard,

267
00:18:02,381 --> 00:18:03,640
and you'll be cutting something,

268
00:18:03,641 --> 00:18:04,880
and you'll hear some loud noises,

269
00:18:04,881 --> 00:18:06,980
and you just want to be able to catch something.

270
00:18:06,981 --> 00:18:16,980
Despite the weather, Alaska Ocean's deck crew continues to haul in 100 to 150 ton nets of

271
00:18:16,981 --> 00:18:21,980
quality, as often as five times a day.

272
00:18:21,981 --> 00:18:27,980
A little too much, not enough though, I guess.

273
00:18:27,981 --> 00:18:33,380
Working the trawl deck has its hazards, but they pale when compared to the conditions

274
00:18:33,381 --> 00:18:40,000
in the factory where one false move can cost you a finger or far worse one man

275
00:18:40,001 --> 00:18:44,840
knows exactly how dangerous the factory can be

276
00:18:47,620 --> 00:18:53,060
the 1.4 million tons of Alaska Pollock caught each year make up 40 percent of

277
00:18:53,061 --> 00:18:57,760
all fish hauled in by US commercial fishermen

278
00:19:03,380 --> 00:19:13,720
Six days into Alaska Ocean's quest to fill its quota, the weather turns mild.

279
00:19:13,721 --> 00:19:18,420
Alaska Ocean has traveled 450 miles northwest of Dutch Harbor.

280
00:19:18,421 --> 00:19:24,100
The smooth sea bottom here, east of Prevenance Canyon, is an area that Captain Scott Simons

281
00:19:24,101 --> 00:19:28,260
knows is historically rich with fish.

282
00:19:28,261 --> 00:19:32,460
Records from past seasons help Scott decide where to set his nets.

283
00:19:32,461 --> 00:19:39,480
put all this information into a database but you know Scott he knows what's going on whether it's

284
00:19:39,481 --> 00:19:48,540
based on information or instinct Scott's decision to fish here is good around the clock the ships

285
00:19:48,541 --> 00:19:55,740
pulling in large halls missions been really good we hauled back once already for 125 tons since

286
00:19:55,741 --> 00:20:02,420
I've been on watch me fall down this thing

287
00:20:03,200 --> 00:20:10,340
but Scott can only catch as much Pollock as the factory below decks can process

288
00:20:10,341 --> 00:20:14,740
stretching nearly the length of the ship the processing plant converts raw

289
00:20:14,741 --> 00:20:21,620
Pollock into packaged frozen product at the rate of 750 fish per minute

290
00:20:21,621 --> 00:20:32,614
By processing aboard ship, Alaska Ocean is better able to hit its quota by spending more time with its nets in the water and less time ferrying its catch back to

291
00:20:32,615 --> 00:20:33,980
shoreside factories.

292
00:20:34,380 --> 00:20:42,500
The Pollock enter the plant through one of three fish bins that each hold 100 tons, the equivalent weight of an adult blue whale.

293
00:20:43,520 --> 00:20:49,700
It'll only take the factory five hours to empty the bin, with the crew working killer 15-hour shifts.

294
00:20:51,620 --> 00:20:56,680
Of the 130-man crew, 100 work in the factory.

295
00:21:00,360 --> 00:21:06,720
The key is automation, but the machinery that makes the factory efficient also makes it dangerous.

296
00:21:07,380 --> 00:21:11,700
The factory is a very dangerous place to work because everything's moving.

297
00:21:12,340 --> 00:21:17,600
Just in the wet end alone, we have 112 conveyors that you could easily step in.

298
00:21:17,880 --> 00:21:20,720
We have knives that are razor-sharp everywhere.

299
00:21:20,721 --> 00:21:25,520
machines everywhere everywhere you look there's something spinning this year we

300
00:21:25,521 --> 00:21:29,720
had a co-worker fall down the forward ladder to our cargo hold and he compound

301
00:21:29,721 --> 00:21:36,040
fractured his back and broke both ankles I've seen lots of severed fingers broken

302
00:21:36,041 --> 00:21:39,920
bones but usually if they lose a finger out here we can't get them to land fast

303
00:21:39,921 --> 00:21:44,900
enough to save the finger so usually if it's cut off it's usually gone

304
00:21:44,901 --> 00:21:50,140
Factory manager Kenichi Yoshida knows how hazardous the processing plant can be.

305
00:21:50,141 --> 00:21:55,480
His left forearm was badly mangled aboard a Japanese factory ship and was

306
00:21:55,481 --> 00:21:58,140
amputated at sea without anesthetic.

307
00:21:58,141 --> 00:22:00,420
It's almost 20 years ago.

308
00:22:00,421 --> 00:22:04,660
Fortunately we had a medical officer in Japanese boat.

309
00:22:04,661 --> 00:22:11,900
Of course, you know, something I cannot do still, but I know how to deal with.

310
00:22:11,901 --> 00:22:18,580
Yoshida supervises Alaska Ocean's factory to keep it a safe workplace, starting with

311
00:22:18,581 --> 00:22:23,880
the weighing and sorting area, where observer Ted Jackson of the National Marine Fisheries

312
00:22:23,881 --> 00:22:25,880
Service has his hands full.

313
00:22:25,881 --> 00:22:32,940
My job aboard the ship as an observer is to basically collect data on the catch.

314
00:22:32,941 --> 00:22:37,980
Every hull that we receive, I randomly select approximately 20 collets.

315
00:22:37,981 --> 00:22:42,980
To reach fish, I determine the sex and record its length in centimeters.

316
00:22:42,981 --> 00:22:48,980
I transmit these figures to management, which get an overview of what's being caught and

317
00:22:48,981 --> 00:22:50,980
how much it weighs.

318
00:22:50,981 --> 00:22:55,980
I collect everything that's not pollock, which is referred to as bycatch.

319
00:22:55,981 --> 00:23:01,980
I identify it to species, I count it, weigh it, and record it all.

320
00:23:01,981 --> 00:23:08,460
anything other than pollock and cod is just is discarded simply they just can't

321
00:23:08,461 --> 00:23:14,880
use it the bycatch can range wide from Alaskan skate to a 12-foot sleeper shark

322
00:23:14,881 --> 00:23:19,980
one of the largest fish the ship has ever landed but big fish aren't the only

323
00:23:19,981 --> 00:23:24,360
unusual items to turn up in Alaska oceans nets you never know what you're

324
00:23:24,361 --> 00:23:27,600
gonna get I mean there's there is a lot of garbage down there to make a long

325
00:23:27,601 --> 00:23:34,980
story short sinks washes dryers oh yeah yeah amazing what people throw away tires the worst

326
00:23:34,981 --> 00:23:40,980
thing to possibly come up in that net though is a whale carcass you'll never smell anything worse

327
00:23:40,981 --> 00:23:48,060
in your life nothing much more unusual than a big mouth sculpting finds its way below decks

328
00:23:48,061 --> 00:23:54,540
production supervisor Travis Fitz troubleshoots problems that arise in the cramped factory

329
00:23:57,600 --> 00:24:01,360
when they're walking through the factory and look down so they don't trip over anything.

330
00:24:01,640 --> 00:24:04,200
I'm looking up to make sure I'm not hitting my head on anything.

331
00:24:04,920 --> 00:24:07,580
I hit my head every day on a day-to-day basis.

332
00:24:09,200 --> 00:24:13,400
To move through the factory, the pollock are automatically sorted by size,

333
00:24:13,640 --> 00:24:16,780
with smaller fish following through widening gaps between rollers.

334
00:24:17,800 --> 00:24:22,540
So each one of these conveyors here goes to a different machine.

335
00:24:23,320 --> 00:24:26,120
Dreamy takes the smaller fish, Filet takes the bigger fish.

336
00:24:27,600 --> 00:24:31,420
One of the hardest jobs in the processing plant is the driver.

337
00:24:32,420 --> 00:24:35,040
We're calling onto this conveyor in front of him,

338
00:24:35,800 --> 00:24:37,560
driving the fish into the machine,

339
00:24:37,760 --> 00:24:40,320
making sure all the fish are facing the same way

340
00:24:40,321 --> 00:24:42,200
so that we don't have any miscuts.

341
00:24:43,200 --> 00:24:45,020
I personally could not drive.

342
00:24:45,120 --> 00:24:46,500
I've tried it. I can't do it.

343
00:24:46,800 --> 00:24:48,480
I can make it maybe 15 minutes,

344
00:24:48,560 --> 00:24:49,980
and my back is just killing me.

345
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:53,740
It takes a long time to become a good driver,

346
00:24:53,741 --> 00:24:59,760
to get over the pain of just standing there for 15 hours feeding fish.

347
00:25:00,700 --> 00:25:04,380
The driver aligns the fish for the machine that will head and gut them.

348
00:25:05,420 --> 00:25:08,880
It can cut the heads off 150 pollock per minute.

349
00:25:09,600 --> 00:25:12,360
A gut extractor then pushes the innards from the fish

350
00:25:12,361 --> 00:25:14,380
before sending them to the fillet machine.

351
00:25:15,440 --> 00:25:19,720
First, two razor-sharp blades make a belly cut down the center of the fish.

352
00:25:20,220 --> 00:25:22,400
Two more cut the flesh from the ribcage

353
00:25:22,401 --> 00:25:24,400
before another pair removes the ribs.

354
00:25:25,580 --> 00:25:28,180
A fourth set of blades cuts out the backbone,

355
00:25:28,340 --> 00:25:30,880
and a final pair slices off the dorsal fin

356
00:25:30,881 --> 00:25:33,080
and creates two individual fillets.

357
00:25:33,780 --> 00:25:36,480
To keep pace with a pollock being hauled in on deck,

358
00:25:36,740 --> 00:25:39,040
five filleting machines work at once,

359
00:25:39,480 --> 00:25:42,360
processing 45,000 fish per hour.

360
00:25:43,060 --> 00:25:44,760
This is the candling table.

361
00:25:45,720 --> 00:25:47,920
There's light shining up through the conveyor belt

362
00:25:47,921 --> 00:25:50,320
so they can see through the fillet any defects

363
00:25:50,321 --> 00:25:53,000
like parasite, bone, skin.

364
00:25:53,580 --> 00:25:57,040
Filets are weighed, then packed in uniform trays for freezing.

365
00:25:59,700 --> 00:26:02,960
Smaller filets will be minced and used to produce cerimi.

366
00:26:04,400 --> 00:26:06,180
So this is called cerimi land.

367
00:26:06,740 --> 00:26:08,700
This is where we make all our cerimi.

368
00:26:09,220 --> 00:26:15,020
Cerimi is just the fish meat that's been washed and bleached in cold, cold, fresh water.

369
00:26:15,021 --> 00:26:20,860
It's been refined to make sure that there's no skin left on it, no bone left in it.

370
00:26:20,861 --> 00:26:22,900
All that's left is just the paste, that's it.

371
00:26:22,901 --> 00:26:30,900
After it's gone through the screw press, it basically has the same consistency as Play-Doh.

372
00:26:30,901 --> 00:26:37,580
The bland taste of cerimi allows it to be flavored to mimic other more expensive seafood

373
00:26:37,581 --> 00:26:38,580
products.

374
00:26:38,581 --> 00:26:44,420
Most people never think they've eaten cerimi, but if you've ever had artificial crab, that's

375
00:26:44,421 --> 00:26:45,420
Surimi.

376
00:26:45,421 --> 00:26:54,400
Surimi may be a staple food in Japan, but the real Pollock delicacy is roe.

377
00:26:54,401 --> 00:26:59,660
Fish guts go to a sorting line where roe, the eggs of the fish, are separated.

378
00:26:59,661 --> 00:27:06,300
Roe is the most valuable part of Pollock, selling in Japan for $60 a pound.

379
00:27:06,301 --> 00:27:11,100
Earnings from filet and surimi just cover the cost of operations.

380
00:27:11,101 --> 00:27:16,760
Remarkably, it's Pollock Row sold at auction in Seattle that earns the ship's profits for an entire year.

381
00:27:17,540 --> 00:27:20,000
Little of the Pollock goes to waste on Alaska Ocean.

382
00:27:21,020 --> 00:27:25,200
The discarded guts and heads are sent one deck below to the fish meal plant,

383
00:27:25,340 --> 00:27:28,680
where the overpowering stench of cooked fish guts fills the air.

384
00:27:29,520 --> 00:27:31,080
We're here in the fish meal plant.

385
00:27:32,040 --> 00:27:37,700
Now down here, you can see, this is where all the fish heads and fish guts go.

386
00:27:37,701 --> 00:27:41,200
So eventually all this right here gets processed into what we call fish meal.

387
00:27:41,201 --> 00:27:43,040
It also produces fish oil.

388
00:27:44,220 --> 00:27:46,540
This is what the finished product looks like.

389
00:27:47,220 --> 00:27:48,000
Real fine.

390
00:27:49,020 --> 00:27:52,660
Now this is what they use for fertilizer, for fish food also.

391
00:27:53,480 --> 00:27:55,080
It's all about efficiency.

392
00:27:56,760 --> 00:27:59,240
That's the name of the game on this boat.

393
00:27:59,720 --> 00:28:04,660
You can take a fish swimming in the ocean in less than 12 hours,

394
00:28:04,661 --> 00:28:10,820
turn it into food for people and feed for animals.

395
00:28:11,540 --> 00:28:13,380
There's virtually no waste.

396
00:28:13,860 --> 00:28:18,460
The 12,000 gallons of fish oil that's produced each day from heads and guts

397
00:28:18,461 --> 00:28:23,640
is combined with bunker oil to fire the boiler that provides heat for the fish meal dryer.

398
00:28:25,120 --> 00:28:27,900
Steam from that same boiler goes to the fresh water maker

399
00:28:27,901 --> 00:28:32,700
that distills thousands of gallons of salt water required to manufacture cerime.

400
00:28:34,660 --> 00:28:38,660
To keep the factory running takes a team of specialized engineers.

401
00:28:39,460 --> 00:28:41,800
There's probably 500 pieces of equipment down there,

402
00:28:41,900 --> 00:28:44,160
couple motors, gearboxes, pumps.

403
00:28:44,820 --> 00:28:48,160
There's a lot of equipment to keep maintained and running.

404
00:28:48,620 --> 00:28:51,520
The factory's saltwater environment may be rugged,

405
00:28:51,820 --> 00:28:53,940
but so are the men and women who crew the ship.

406
00:28:54,840 --> 00:28:58,140
The lives of Alaska Ocean's fishermen are filled with risk.

407
00:28:59,080 --> 00:29:00,540
Of course, it's always in the back of your mind.

408
00:29:00,600 --> 00:29:02,300
You think about it. It happens out here.

409
00:29:02,301 --> 00:29:07,040
but we try to keep this a very, very safe vessel.

410
00:29:07,041 --> 00:29:09,800
We're constantly on safety issues.

411
00:29:09,801 --> 00:29:12,940
So we do the drills, the emergency musters.

412
00:29:12,941 --> 00:29:14,540
So we do the fire drills.

413
00:29:14,541 --> 00:29:16,740
You know, we do the man overboard drills.

414
00:29:16,741 --> 00:29:19,060
Man overboard, port side.

415
00:29:19,061 --> 00:29:20,660
Man overboard, port side.

416
00:29:20,661 --> 00:29:22,960
This is a drill. This is a drill.

417
00:29:25,960 --> 00:29:29,400
Jaime Montelongo, who supervises the drill,

418
00:29:29,401 --> 00:29:31,460
knows its importance firsthand.

419
00:29:31,461 --> 00:29:42,460
The skiff flipped so I went in the water and tried to hop me back on and tried to get on and I couldn't do it.

420
00:29:42,461 --> 00:29:47,460
The survival time for a man in sub 32 degree water is 15 minutes.

421
00:29:47,461 --> 00:29:50,589
The waves kept on hitting me, bouncing me back off.

422
00:29:50,590 --> 00:29:57,460
So after about 7 minutes in the water, the captain sent the mate down and the water pulled me back in the skiff.

423
00:29:57,461 --> 00:30:01,460
Safety aboard ship also has a practical side.

424
00:30:01,461 --> 00:30:06,460
If people are getting hurt, you're not making, you're going to have trouble making money.

425
00:30:06,461 --> 00:30:11,460
If you have to take somebody in, you know, it's a two and a half day trip out here and

426
00:30:11,461 --> 00:30:13,460
two and a half day trip back in.

427
00:30:13,461 --> 00:30:18,460
You know, burning 10,000 gallons of fuel a day, plus everybody else that you're paying,

428
00:30:18,461 --> 00:30:20,460
so it pays to be safe.

429
00:30:20,461 --> 00:30:29,340
Time is truly money aboard a factory troll where profit is measured and how quickly a

430
00:30:29,341 --> 00:30:33,080
quota is met.

431
00:30:33,081 --> 00:30:38,280
But 15 days into Alaska Ocean's final trip of the season, the factory pace has slowed

432
00:30:38,281 --> 00:30:39,840
to a crawl.

433
00:30:39,841 --> 00:30:43,340
No fish.

434
00:30:43,341 --> 00:30:47,680
Up on the bridge, Captain Scott Simons feels the pressure.

435
00:30:47,681 --> 00:30:51,480
He scans his down sounder for any positive signs.

436
00:30:51,481 --> 00:30:52,340
So they're around us.

437
00:30:52,341 --> 00:30:55,040
It's just a matter of if you see something really solid

438
00:30:55,041 --> 00:30:56,580
on the sonar, you would turn for it.

439
00:30:56,581 --> 00:30:58,800
But otherwise, you go crazy trying to chase

440
00:30:58,801 --> 00:30:59,860
each individual little bump.

441
00:30:59,861 --> 00:31:01,440
You just can't do it.

442
00:31:01,441 --> 00:31:03,140
There's so many square miles of water,

443
00:31:03,141 --> 00:31:08,920
and this net is relatively small in the big picture.

444
00:31:08,921 --> 00:31:11,120
If Scott doesn't find fish soon,

445
00:31:11,121 --> 00:31:14,340
the last trip of the season may be a long and costly one

446
00:31:14,341 --> 00:31:15,340
for Alaska Ocean.

447
00:31:17,680 --> 00:31:22,940
Over half of all the seafood landed in the United States is caught in Alaskan waters.

448
00:31:34,480 --> 00:31:37,960
In its search to find fish to fill its season's quota,

449
00:31:38,440 --> 00:31:41,240
Alaska Ocean travels further and further from port.

450
00:31:41,940 --> 00:31:44,400
It's now at the northernmost point of its voyage,

451
00:31:44,900 --> 00:31:47,260
525 miles from Dutch Harbor.

452
00:31:47,680 --> 00:31:51,320
and 47 miles from the edge of Russian territory.

453
00:31:51,321 --> 00:31:53,460
Because the bottom of the Bering Sea here

454
00:31:53,461 --> 00:31:55,440
is relatively flat and shallow,

455
00:31:55,441 --> 00:31:58,460
ranging in depths from 300 to 500 feet,

456
00:31:58,461 --> 00:31:59,900
it's ideal for trawling.

457
00:32:01,280 --> 00:32:04,860
Alaska Ocean still needs over 1,000 tons of pollock

458
00:32:04,861 --> 00:32:06,760
to send the crew home for the season.

459
00:32:07,680 --> 00:32:09,400
But there's one problem.

460
00:32:09,401 --> 00:32:13,120
Alaska Ocean's nets are idle, no fish.

461
00:32:14,500 --> 00:32:16,160
Well, by the time you think you figure it all out,

462
00:32:16,161 --> 00:32:17,560
they're gone.

463
00:32:17,561 --> 00:32:19,920
I may have to make a more aggressive turn

464
00:32:19,921 --> 00:32:22,620
and try to go a different direction through the area.

465
00:32:23,340 --> 00:32:24,580
But this right now, so far,

466
00:32:24,640 --> 00:32:26,460
the catch rate is much less than it was the last tow.

467
00:32:26,540 --> 00:32:27,740
I had better sign last tow.

468
